{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close for days 1–10, but become increasingly too low from ~day 20 onward, especially in rising regimes (systematic underprediction of late-horizon upside).",
    "Trend/acceleration miss: when the market transitions into a strong uptrend (late Nov), the model fails to capture acceleration; apply an upward drift adjustment that increases with horizon when recent returns are positive (e.g., scale-up days 15–30).",
    "Over-smoothing/mean-reversion bias: the model dampens large moves; after a breakout it predicts partial reversion while realized prices continue trending. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 5–10 days show persistent higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Spike handling: the model tends to under-forecast post-spike continuation (it treats spikes as temporary). If a spike is accompanied by follow-through the next 1–3 sessions, shift the entire remaining path upward rather than fading it.",
    "Regime switch heuristic: if the last observed price is above the 20–30d moving average and the MA slope is positive, add a positive level shift that grows with horizon (small at 1–5d, larger at 20–30d). Conversely, in downtrends, apply the symmetric downward shift (not evidenced strongly here).",
    "Late-horizon variance too low: forecast distribution is too tight vs realized; widen the range (allow higher highs/lower lows) beyond ~2 weeks, with more upside allowance in bullish regimes."
  ]
}
